WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.620
Film Courage: How important is it for you
to have a strong personal connection to the

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:04.640
material that you’re writing?

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.910
Gary Goldstein: I think it’s really important.
I think it’s so important. If you’re writing

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:13.610
something for you like a play (a stage play)
or something that’s personal. Obviously.

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:17.690
Absolutely. But even on something that’s
not particularly about you or something of

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:21.670
your life or your concern, you have to find
your way into it and you have to find your

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:24.630
way, how do you connect into it?

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:30.410
What makes you the unique writer of any story
or any script is your perspective and what

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:35.440
you bring to it. If you and I were to write
the same script, we might get to the same

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.899
end goal and the story might not be similar.
But how we get there would be very different

00:00:39.899 --> 00:00:44.800
because we’d be bringing our own experiences
to it and our own views of things and other

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.839
people’s views of things that we’ve seen,
you know.

00:00:47.839 --> 00:00:52.819
So I think being able to connect, to find
stories that you connect with or find great

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:57.690
connections that you relate to because it
becomes very honest that way and so whether

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:02.989
it’s comedy or drama, honesty and real experiences
and (like what we talked about earlier) things

00:01:02.989 --> 00:01:07.329
that you overhear in the supermarket, that
you can use because that moved you or made

00:01:07.329 --> 00:01:13.619
you laugh and you can use that, those connections
are really critical I think to make good writing,

00:01:13.619 --> 00:01:18.880
for good stories. You don’t have to but
I definitely think it’s a big help.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:20.960
Film Courage: Why did you begin writing?

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:30.969
Gary Goldstein: Ahhh…that’s a good question.
You know, I think I started writing…my very

00:01:30.969 --> 00:01:34.189
first writing was when I wrote movie reviews
for the high school newspaper. And I think

00:01:34.189 --> 00:01:39.420
part of it was there was a certain escape
factor to it back then. Movies were a good

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.600
escape and writing about them was just…I
had a lot of thoughts about them.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:50.719
I loved movies and I read a lot of film criticism
and it was really a big part of my world and

00:01:50.719 --> 00:01:54.159
it meant a lot to me. And it was like “Oh,
I want to do that. I can write that. And I’ve

00:01:54.159 --> 00:01:57.320
read so much and I’ve seen so many movies,
it’s fun. Let me take a crack at it.”

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:01.549
And it was fun and I liked being able to share
opinions and get conversations going about

00:02:01.549 --> 00:02:06.509
things and hear people who had different opinions
than mine and all of that and again I just

00:02:06.509 --> 00:02:11.950
loved writing the words and crafting the words
and it just sort of evolved and I’ve always

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:17.420
loved movies and theater, and television and
stories and books and telling stories and

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:19.450
reading stories and all of the so.

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:25.040
And at a certain point if you want to be part
of that, you are either on one side or another.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:30.860
So I started as a a publicist promoting other
people’s movies and being able to talk about

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:34.500
other people’s movies, writing and selling
other people’s movies.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.980
But then at a certain point I realized I wanted
to start telling those stories. I wanted to

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:43.480
be the one to create those movies and those
stories. That started speaking to me much

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:50.720
more then being on the other side of the table.
So it just sort of evolved.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:56.520
And also it’s something that ultimately
I think I have more of talent for than other

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:00.379
things. So you go where you have the most
talent, the most ability.

00:03:00.379 --> 00:03:03.870
Film Courage: Do you remember the defining
moment where you said “I think I want to

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:05.780
leave the publicity world?”

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:06.780
Gary Goldstein: Yes I do!

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:07.870
Film Courage: Oh…you do? There was a defining
moment?

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:13.450
Gary Goldstein: Yes, exactly. I was there
like it was yesterday. My boss was leaving

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:19.930
to go on a trip for three weeks. And she said
to me “You they want you to sign a contract

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:24.900
here.” Because I think I was at a place
where they were going to promote me (give

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:28.750
me a little bit of a promotion or whatever,
some title promotion and give me some new

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:30.430
duties or something.

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:33.810
And she said “So they want you to sign a
contract.” “Well how long is the contract

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:38.219
for?” And she said “Well, it’s a 6-year
contract. It’s 2 years, 2 years and 2 years.”

00:03:38.219 --> 00:03:41.632
And so she said “While I’m away, read
it, take a look at it and sign it. And then

00:03:41.632 --> 00:03:44.790
when I come back, I’ll sign it and we’ll
move forward."

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:50.519
And when she was gone I was like “I can’t
sign that contract?” And I just realized

00:03:50.519 --> 00:03:55.670
that I didn’t want to be there. It just
wasn’t right for me. It was a tie and jacket

00:03:55.670 --> 00:04:02.260
job for me at the time. You know, it was very
political and all of that. And not that screenwriting

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:04.000
doesn’t have its own politics and all of
that.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:10.819
But I don’t know. I just felt like I wasn’t
accomplishing what I felt I wanted to do at

00:04:10.819 --> 00:04:16.040
a young age. I just felt like this wasn’t
where I needed to be. Even though I was doing

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:17.890
well, it was where I needed to be.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:23.860
My father was visiting me around that time.
My father passed away last year but he was

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:29.150
a very bottom-line, kind of money-oriented
guy (very meat and potatoes, not a show biz

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:33.580
guy at all). He always supported what I did
but didn’t have a great sense of it.

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:37.210
But I said “You know, Dad…they want me
to sign a contract. What do you think I should

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:40.180
do?” And he said “Well, do you want to
stay there?” And I said “I don’t.”

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:43.790
And he said “Well, then you should leave.”
And I said “I can’t leave this job. This

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:48.660
is a job that people aspire to.” And he
said “What do you want to do?” And I said

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:52.090
“Actually, I want to write. I want to write
movies and television.” And he said “Then

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:55.891
that’s what you should do.” And I said
“Well, what if it doesn’t work out?”

00:04:55.891 --> 00:05:00.440
And he said “Listen, you don’t want to
be my age and look back and say I should have

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:04.070
done it and then it’s too late.” He said
“If that’s what you have to do, you have

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:07.790
to do it.” And I was like shocked that he
said that. And not that I always listen to

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:12.110
him but it was coming from an I interesting
place. And I just thought about it and I said

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:15.450
“He’s right. I’m not going to sign that
contract.”

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:19.250
And my boss came back and she said “So did
you sign that contract?” And I said “No.”

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:22.210
And she said “Oh? So you just didn’t get
to it yet?” And I said “I’m not signing

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:25.710
that contract.” And she said “You’re
not signing that contract?” And I said “No.

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:29.890
I’m leaving. I’m going to write for television.”
And I did. And then I called my father and

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:34.240
said “Dad, I quit my job.” And he said
“You did what? How could you quit your job?

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:38.750
Who quits a job like that?” And you know,
for years after that he’s say to me “You

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:41.990
know if you stayed there, you probably would
have been president of that place.

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:47.340
But I was just so glad to be gone and it was
a good experience for certain things. It certainly

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:53.090
helped me in many, many ways as a writer,
as a marketer of my material but that was

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:55.560
the time that I knew I had to move on. And
I never looked back.

00:05:55.560 --> 00:06:01.700
Film Courage: So when you got your things
together in a box or whatever, did you feel

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:02.700
awake?

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:05.810
Gary Goldstein: I felt so great. When I was
saying goodbye to people… It was really

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:09.310
funny and I don’t mean to be arrogant about
it or anything because it was a difficult

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:15.330
time work wise and personally at that time
but I just kind of needed to go. And I remember

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:20.990
one of the people I worked with he said to
me “You know, what you’re doing is so

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:27.870
brave to leave.” And I said “No, it would
be more courageous to stay.” Meaning that

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:35.380
it was tougher to stay than to go in a certain
respect but I didn’t realize what a deep

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:41.900
pool I was diving into becoming a writer.
Like I said I was just so optimistic about

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:48.440
it, I wasn’t quite aware of the difficulties
of it. And so for me it was just the thing

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:52.850
to do. Staying there would have been more
difficult. But I was happy to go. I was done.

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:57.430
Film Courage: Do you think a lot of people
do that in Hollywood? They stay in certain

00:06:57.430 --> 00:06:58.430
things because…

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:02.360
Gary Goldstein: Everybody in life stays in
things that they are just not happy with because

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:06.850
sometimes they just don’t know where to
go. I’ve been very fortunate. I’ve kind

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:12.720
of always known what I’ve wanted to do.
I have things that I’m passionate about.

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:18.800
My work is my enjoyment. So I feel very, very
fortunate for that.

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:21.850
But a lot of people aren’t happy in a job
but they don’t really know what else they

00:07:21.850 --> 00:07:26.810
want to do. And I would never recommend someone
not be happy with something and just up and

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:30.070
leave without knowing where they are going
because it could be a very long time to get

00:07:30.070 --> 00:07:34.620
another job and you are always worth valuable
while you’re employed (whatever you’re

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:35.620
doing).

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:43.640
Yeah but I think there are definitely people
living in those desperate situations but I

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:48.130
encourage people who really aren’t happy
in what they do to find their passion and

00:07:48.130 --> 00:07:52.830
to figure out a way to make it a career if
you can. Not everything can be. Not everything

00:07:52.830 --> 00:08:00.070
is that monetizable or that realistic but
work is a big part of our lives and it really

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:01.090
helps if you can enjoy it.

